[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The mswitch1 uplink trunk is healthy; the outage is upstream of Location A, not on the trunk. Evidence:
- Intra-site L2/L3 are fine: hosts on VLAN 10/20 reach their gateways on router0 (pc4->172.16.10.1 and laptop0->172.16.20.1 succeed) and inter-VLAN also works (pc4<->laptop0 pings succeed).
- mswitch1 is correctly trunking VLAN 10/20 on both uplink legs. It uses 802.1Q subinterfaces bridged per-VLAN: br_vlan10/br_vlan20 have eth0.10 and eth4.10/.20 in forwarding state (bridge link show). Per-port counters on eth0.10/eth4.10 and eth0.20/eth4.20 show active RX/TX (ip -s link), proving tags are passing across eth0 (to switch1) and eth4 (to router0). There is no congestion/drop indicated.
- Router0 has the VLAN SVIs up (eth0.10=172.16.10.1, eth0.20=172.16.20.1) and learns ARP/MACs from Location A (ip neigh shows pc4/pc5/laptop0).
- The failure appears when leaving Location A: from pc4 to 192.168.200.11 returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” from 172.16.10.1 (router0 has no route for 192.168.200.0/24), and even to Location C (172.18.51.0/24) router0 itself cannot ping 172.18.51.16 despite having a route via 10.16.1.2; however router0 can ping 10.16.1.2, local WAN next-hop. This localizes the break beyond router0 (e.g., missing/incorrect routes or ACLs upstream), not on the mswitch1 trunk.

Root cause: Upstream routing from router0 toward external networks (e.g., 192.168.200.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24) is missing or broken; the mswitch1 trunk is not dropping traffic.

Corrective fix: Restore correct routing on the WAN/core (router1 and beyond): install/advertise routes for external prefixes and verify return paths and ACLs. No changes needed on mswitch1; its relevant ports eth0 and eth4 (and subifs .10/.20) are functioning properly.
=== END ===